---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Doc from '../components/legal/Doc.astro';
const updated = '30 October 2026';
// Shows a draft note and keeps the page out of search until the placeholders are filled (design/legal/README.md).
const draft = true;
---

<Base title="Brand agreement · Comarket" description="The agreement for brands on Comarket: fees per completed sale, the new-customer rule, refunds, billing, creators, brand assets and liability." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Brand agreement"
    lede="You pay only for completed sales from new customers. This is the agreement behind that."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/brand-terms"
  >
    <div class="short">
      <b>The short version</b>
      <ul>
        <li>Nothing upfront, nothing monthly. On each completed sale you pay the creator rate you set, plus our fee: 15% of the new customer's first payment and 10% of renewals, for 12 months. After that the customer is all yours.</li>
        <li>A sale counts only if the buyer is new to you, checked against your Stripe customers by email, phone and card.</li>
        <li>Refunds inside your refund window, lost chargebacks and fraud cost you nothing.</li>
        <li>You approve every creator. You can leave on 30 days' notice.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>1. This agreement</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.1</span>This agreement is between [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]] ("Comarket", "we") and the business that accepts it
      when opening a brand account ("you"). The person accepting confirms they can bind the business. The general terms in
      <a href="/terms#general-terms">section 7 of the Terms of use</a> and the <a href="/dpa">data processing addendum</a> (the
      "DPA") are part of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.2</span>Comarket works with web checkouts through Stripe only.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Offer:</b> what you ask creators to promote: the product, what buyers get (such as a trial or discount), the Creator Rate, and the Refund Window.</li>
      <li><b>Creator:</b> someone who has accepted the <a href="/creator-terms">Creator Agreement</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Storefront:</b> the page we host for a creator you approved, in your look with their name on it.</li>
      <li><b>Attributed Checkout:</b> a checkout in your connected Stripe account that a buyer starts from a Storefront.</li>
      <li><b>First Payment:</b> the first successful payment above zero from an Attributed Checkout, including the first payment after a free trial that started there.</li>
      <li><b>Renewal:</b> each later successful payment by the same customer for the Offer's product (including its plans, upgrades and add-ons) during the Commission Period.</li>
      <li><b>Commission Period:</b> the 12 months from the First Payment.</li>
      <li><b>Net Amount:</b> what the buyer paid, after discounts, excluding sales tax, less any refund. Stripe's fees are not deducted.</li>
      <li><b>Creator Rate:</b> what you pay the creator, set in the Offer as a percentage of the Net Amount or a fixed amount per sale.</li>
      <li><b>Refund Window:</b> the refund period you set on each Offer, counted from each payment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Setting up</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.1</span><b>Stripe.</b> You connect your Stripe account and give us read access to your customers, payments,
      subscriptions, refunds and disputes. We never move money in your account or change it. Keep it connected, and don't
      remove the Storefront reference from Attributed Checkouts, until the last Commission Period ends.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.2</span><b>Offers.</b> You set each Offer. You can change or end it at any time; a change applies to checkouts
      started after creators are told.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.3</span><b>Creators.</b> No Storefront goes live until you approve the creator and its design. You may
      decline any creator, and withdraw an approval at any time; we take the Storefront down within 2 business days. Creators
      may also decline or stop promoting your Offer. Every Storefront states that the creator earns a commission.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.4</span>We don't promise any number of creators, posts or sales.
    </p>

    <h2>4. What counts as a sale</h2>
    <p><span class="n">4.1</span>A sale counts when:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>the buyer started an Attributed Checkout from a Storefront;</li>
      <li>the First Payment succeeded within 30 days of the buyer's last visit to that Storefront;</li>
      <li>the buyer is a new customer (4.2);</li>
      <li>the First Payment wasn't fully refunded within its Refund Window; and</li>
      <li>it isn't excluded (4.3).</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.2</span><b>New customers only.</b> A buyer is not new if their email, phone number or card fingerprint (a code
      Stripe derives from a card, never the card number) matches any customer in your Stripe account from before the checkout
      started. One match is enough. If none of the three is available, the sale counts unless you show us the buyer was already
      your customer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.3</span><b>Excluded.</b> A sale doesn't count if the buyer is the creator, someone in their household or
      acting for them; if it came from conduct the Creator Agreement forbids; or if it's a test or a fraudulent payment.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.4</span>The Storefront that started the checkout gets the credit. Sales are never split between creators.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Fees</h2>
    <p><span class="n">5.1</span>For each sale that counts, you pay:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>the Creator Rate</b> on the First Payment and each Renewal, unless the Offer limits it to the First Payment; and</li>
      <li><b>our fee:</b> 15% of the Net Amount of the First Payment and 10% of the Net Amount of each Renewal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.2</span>Both stop when the Commission Period ends. Nothing is owed on that customer after 12 months.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.3</span>There is no setup fee, subscription or minimum. Sales that don't count cost nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.4</span>We may change our fee with 30 days' notice. A change applies only to checkouts started after it takes
      effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.5</span>Fees are in US dollars and exclude any sales tax, which you pay where it applies.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Refunds, chargebacks and fraud</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.1</span>A payment fully refunded within its Refund Window owes nothing; a partial refund reduces the Creator
      Rate and our fee in proportion. Because we only bill payments whose Refund Window has closed, you won't pay for these.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.2</span>If a buyer disputes a payment with their bank within 120 days and you lose, the Creator Rate and our
      fee on it are reversed. Fees on a sale excluded under 4.3 are reversed whenever we find out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.3</span>Other refunds after the Refund Window don't reverse fees.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.4</span>We credit reversals on your next invoice, or refund them within 30 days if this agreement has ended.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Billing</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.1</span>Early each month we invoice every payment on a counted sale whose Refund Window closed in the previous
      month. The invoice lists each payment by its Stripe ID, with its date, Net Amount, creator, Creator Rate, our fee and any
      reversals. We charge the payment method you keep on file through Stripe on the invoice date.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.2</span>If a charge fails and isn't fixed within 14 days of our reminder, we may pause your Storefronts until
      it is. Checkouts started before the pause still count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.3</span><b>Creator payments.</b> Each creator appoints us as their limited agent to collect the Creator Rate
      from you. When you pay us, your debt to the creator for that amount is settled. Don't pay creators directly for counted
      sales.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.4</span><b>Questions.</b> You may dispute an invoice line within 30 days by telling us which line and why. A
      person reviews it and replies within 30 days; if we agree, we credit it on your next invoice. Pay the undisputed part on
      time. Lines not disputed within 30 days are final, except for fraud or obvious error.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Your responsibilities</h2>
    <p><span class="n">8.1</span>You are responsible for your products, prices, checkout, delivery, customer support, refund policy and the laws that apply to selling them. You must:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>make sure every claim in your Offer, and every claim you let creators make, is true and backed by evidence;</li>
      <li>never ask a creator to hide their connection to you or to make claims you haven't approved, and tell us if you see a post that does (under the FTC's Endorsement Guides, 16 CFR Part 255, advertisers can be liable for their endorsers' posts);</li>
      <li>tell buyers, in your privacy policy, that you use service providers to check new customers and credit referrals; and</li>
      <li>not use Comarket for weapons, drugs, tobacco or vaping, gambling, adult products, prescription medicines, investment or crypto schemes, multi-level marketing, counterfeit goods, or anything Stripe prohibits.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Brand assets and content</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.1</span>You let us use your names, logos, colors, typefaces, product images and copy (including what we read
      from your public website) to build and show Storefronts and our dashboards, and let creators you approve use them to
      promote your Offer, while this agreement lasts. You confirm you have the rights to do so. If you don't have web rights for
      a typeface, tell us and we'll use a similar one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.2</span>We name you as a customer only with your permission. You keep your brand assets; we keep Comarket,
      its software and Storefront designs; creators keep their content. You may show creators' content on your Storefronts, and
      elsewhere only if the creator agrees.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Data</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.1</span>For your Stripe customer data, we are your service provider under the <a href="/dpa">DPA</a>. We
      never give creators your buyers' identities. Our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> covers the rest.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Warranties, indemnity and liability</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.1</span>We'll provide Comarket with reasonable care. Otherwise it is provided "as is", without other
      warranties, and we don't promise it will be uninterrupted or produce sales.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.2</span>You will defend and compensate us for third-party claims arising from your products, your Offers or
      claims, your brand assets, or your dealings with buyers. We will defend and compensate you for third-party claims that
      Comarket's own software infringes their intellectual property.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.3</span>Neither side is liable for lost profits or indirect, incidental or consequential damages. Each side's
      total liability is limited to the fees you paid us in the 12 months before the claim. These limits don't apply to your
      duty to pay fees and Creator Rates, to 11.2, or to fraud or willful misconduct.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Ending</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.1</span>You may end this agreement with 30 days' notice, and pause your Offers at any time. We may end it with
      30 days' notice, or at once for a serious breach, fraud, or non-payment after a reminder, and may pause Storefronts while
      we look into a suspected breach.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.2</span>When it ends, we take your Storefronts down within 10 business days. Creators don't have to delete
      posts already published.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.3</span>Fees and Creator Rates on sales made before the end, including their Renewals until each Commission
      Period ends, stay payable, and we keep billing them monthly. Keep Stripe connected until then.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">12.4</span>Sections 5 to 7, 9.2, 10, 11 and 12 survive the end of this agreement.
    </p>
  </Doc>
  <Footer />
</Base>
